          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+an+operator"} spectrum
            lies between 0 and 1 (hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+operator"} positive operator
            which is
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq 1
            %em effect
            or
            %em quantum effect
            (see e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#Ludwig"}> Ludwig
            ,
            = succeed "))." do
              %a{:href => "#Kraus"} Kraus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+operators"} projection operators
            and may be thought of as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            with “unsharp” or “fuzzy” value.
          %p
            The notion of
            %em effect algebra
            (due to (
            %a{:href => "#FoulisBennet"}> Foulis-Bennet 94
            )) is an abstraction of the structure exhibited by the collection of such effect operators.
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %span.newWikiWord
                partial commutative monoid
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/partial+monoid"} ?
              (PCM) consists of a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              with a zero element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in  M
              and a partial binary operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee : M \times M \to M
              satisfying the three requirements below. They involve the notation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \perp y
              for:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y
              is defined; in that case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
              are called orthogonal.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Commutativity:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\perp y
                  implies
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\perp x
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi y
                          %mo =
                          %mi y
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\vee y=y\vee x
              %li
                %p
                  Associativity:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\perp z
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\perp(y\vee z)
                  implies
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\perp y
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x\vee y)\perp z
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi z
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\vee (y\vee z)=(x\vee y)\vee z
              %li
                %p
                  Zero:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\perp x
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\vee x=x
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#FoulisBennet"} Foulis-Bennet 94
              p.22)
            %p
              In a PCM, we define:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mo ⇔
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y:\Leftrightarrow \exists_z. x \vee z = y
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A PCM is preordered by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p Reflexivity is immediate from the Zero axiom, and transitivity follows easily from Associativity.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em generalized effect algebra
              is a PCM
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, 0, \vee)
              such that:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Cancellation Law: If
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \perp b
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \perp c
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = a \vee c
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo =
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = c
              %li
                %p
                  Positivity Law: If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \perp b
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = 0
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi b
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b = 0
            %p
              In a generalized effect algebra, we define:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                    %mo :
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\ominus x=z:\Leftrightarrow y=x\vee z
              (which exists iff
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A generalized effect algebra is partially ordered by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \le x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee a = y
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \vee b = x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee (a \vee b) = x = x \vee 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = 0
              by the Cancellation Law. Therefore,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b = 0
              and so
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em effect algebra
              is a PCM
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,0,\vee)
              with an orthocomplement. The latter is a unary operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\perp :E\to E
              satisfying:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Orthocomplement Law.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^\perp\in E
                  is the unique element in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  with
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\vee x^\perp=1
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ⊥
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1=0^\perp
              %li
                %p
                  Zero-One Law.
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\perp 1\Rightarrow x=0
            %p
              For such an effect algebra one defines:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⊥
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\wedge y:=(x^\perp\vee y^\perp)^\perp
              (
              %a{:href => "#FoulisBennet"} Foulis-Bennet 94
              p. 23)
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, 0, \vee)
              is an effect algebra iff it is a generalized effect algebra with a greatest element, in which case that greatest element is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = 0^\perp
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, 0, \vee)
              be an effect algebra. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a generalized effect algebra since:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Cancellation Law. If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = a \vee c
                  then
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi b
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi b
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi c
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi b
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b \vee (a \vee b)^\perp = a \vee c \vee (a \vee b)^\perp = 1
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo =
                          %mi c
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi b
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊥
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊥
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = c = (a \vee (a \vee b)^\perp)^\perp
              %li
                %p
                  Positivity Law. If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = 0
                  then
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee b) \perp 1
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \perp 1
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \perp 1
                  by Associativity. Thus,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b = 0
                  by the Zero-One Law.
            %p
              1 is the greatest elements since, for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee x^\perp = 1
              and so
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq 1
            %p
              Conversely, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, 0, \vee)
              be a generalized effect algebra with greatest element 1. Define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^\perp = 1 \ominus x
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Orthocomplement Law.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^\perp
                  is the unique element such that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee x^\perp = 1
                  by definition.
              %li
                %p
                  Zero-One Law. If
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \perp 1
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq x \vee 1
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mn 1
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee 1 = 1
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mn 1
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ∨
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee 1 = 0 \vee 1
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
                  by the Cancellation Law.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              If we consider
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi up
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi down
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\ominus y:up(y)\to down(y^\perp)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi down
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi up
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\vee y:down(y^\perp)\to up
              as functors between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              we have
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((-\wedge y)\dashv (-\ominus y)\dashv (-\vee y)
            %p
              Hence these functors are a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+functor"} frobenius pair
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#FoulisBennet"} Foulis-Bennet 94
              p.25)
          %h2#morphisms Morphisms
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be effect algebras. A
              %em morphism of effect algebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : E \rightarrow F
              is a function such that:
            %ol
              %li
                %p f(1) = 1
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \perp y
                  then
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) \perp f(y)
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x \vee y) = f(x) \vee f(y)
            %p
              We write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi EA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{EA}
              for the category of effect algebras and morphisms of effect algebras.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            (1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effect+algebra+of+predicates"} effect algebra of predicates
          %p
            (2) The real unit inteval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            being addition of real numbers is an effect algebra since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            is a pcm with zero object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and commutative, associative addition of real numbers and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\perp y
            iff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+y\le 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in [0,1]
            is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} x
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \x^\perp=1-x
          %p
            (3) Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            denote the discrete-probability-distribution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            which sends a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the collection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X):=\{r_1 x_1+\dots +r_n x_n|x_i\in X, r_i\in [0,1], \Sigma_i r_i=1\}
          %p
            of formal convex combinations of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(D)
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            which has as objects (just) sets and a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(D)
            is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to D(Y)
            which can be interpreted as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+chain"} Markov chain
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            of the transition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\to x_i
            is the coefficient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_i\in [0,1]
            in the the convex sum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi …
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=r_1 x_1+\dots+r_n x_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(D)
            has as coproducts coproducts of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "is" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Kl(D)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:X\to D(X+X)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [id_X,id_X]\circ p =id_X
            means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x)\in D(X+X)
            is a convex combination of elements of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 2
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_1 x, k_2 x\in X+X
            such that we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 2
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x)=\varphi(x)k_1 x +\psi(x)k_2 x
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x),\psi(x)\in [0,1]
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi{x}+\psi(x)=1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x)
            can be written as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 1
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 2
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x)=\varphi(x)k_1 x + (1-\varphi(x))k_2 x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi:X\to [0,1]
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x)
            is the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\mapsto 1-\varphi(x)
            which is point-wise the orthocomplement of the unit interval in the second example.
          %p
            (4) In the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hilb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hilb
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert spaces
            the coproduct coincides with the product and hence is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:X\to X\otimes X
            on a Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo &gt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p=\lt p_1,p_2\gt
            of a pair of maps and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [id_X,id_X]\circ p
            is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1 +p_2=id_X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            is point-wise addition. In particular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2
            determine each other uniquely.
          %p
            And now comes the eponymous feature: The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hilb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hilb
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger category
            and the dagger morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Hilb
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hilb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\dagger:Hilb^{op}\to Hilb
            is the identity on objects and complex conjugation on morphisms. An endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to X
            is called to be a
            %em positive endomorphism
            if there is a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo †
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g^\dagger\circ g
            and a predicate on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is called to be an
            %strong effect
            (on
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2
            are positive. Another name for effect is “unsharp predicate”; in this terminology a “sharp predicate” is a subset of the set of projections onto
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            (5) In a C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^\ast
            \-algebra the elements between 0 and 1 form an effect algebra with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1-a)
            as the complement of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %p
            (6) As a special case, we obtain the effect algebra of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            %a{:href => "#DeGroote"} De Groote
            defines a
            %em spectral order
            on self-adjoint operators which makes the collection of effects a
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                boundedly complete lattice
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/boundedly+complete+lattice"} ?
          %h2#relation_to_dposets Relation to D-Posets
          %p
            A parallel concept in the literature is that of
            %em D-poset
            (sometimes called
            = succeed ")," do
              %em D-lattice
            %a{:href => "#Kopka"} Kôpka 92
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ChovKop"} Chovanec-Kôpka 95
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A partial binary operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ominus
              on a poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P, \leq)
              is called a
              %em difference operation
              (or simply
              = succeed ")" do
                %em difference
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              iff:
            %p
              (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b \leftrightarrow b \ominus a
              is defined,
            %p
              (2)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \ominus a \leq b
            %p
              (3)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \ominus (b \ominus a) = a
            %p
              (4) if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b \leq c
              implies that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \ominus b \leq c \ominus a
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊖
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c \ominus a) \ominus (c \ominus  b) = b \ominus a
            %p
              A
              %em D-poset
              is a poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P, \leq, \ominus, 1)
              with a difference operation and greatest element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in P
          %p
            Any effect algebra is automatically a D-poset under the difference
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊖
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c := b \ominus a \iff a \oplus c = b
          %h2#relation_to_boolean_algebras Relation to Boolean algebras
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            is an effect algebra, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\bot b
            whenever
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge b=0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^\bot=\neg a
          %p
            Starting from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powerset"} powerset
            Boolean algebra, for example, we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\bot b
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are disjoint, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\vee b
            is the disjoint union.
          %p A function is a Boolean algebra homomorphism if and only if it is a morphism of effect algebras.
          %p
            In fact the finite Boolean algebras form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subcategory"} dense subcategory
            of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi EA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{EA}
            of effect algebras. This means that every effect algebra is a canonical colimit of finite Boolean algebras.
          %p
            Since the powerset functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinBool
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}\to \mathbf{FinBool}^{\mathrm{op}}
            is an equivalence of categories (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality#StoneSpacesAndProfiniteSets"}> Stone duality
            ), this density property also means that we have a full and faithful functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi EA
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:\mathbf{EA}\to [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
            %strong tests
            of an effect algebra. A test is a sequence of orthogonal elements that sum to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi …
                  %mo ∨
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(E) (n) = \{ (a_1,\dots,a_n)\ |\ a_1\vee\dots \vee a_n=1\}
          %p
            For example, starting from the unit interval,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T([0,1])(n)
            gives the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex#TopologicalSimplex"} simplex
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+algebra"} separation algebra
          %div
            %p
